Z||000003||mwbackus||10-21-1999||03:33 AM||||     If someone were to hit my Fiero without insurance, as would appear to be the case in your accident, I would sue the $#^#@ out of them, thereby either getting reimbursed for an extreme excess of the amount required to purchase an equiv. vehicle, or forcing them into a very dark and hideous bankruptcy.<P>     There is no excuse for driving uninsured, and the courts are very favorable and willing to reward "extreme" compensation from the damage caused by these losers.<P>Mike <BR>(Warning : This vehicle protected by "blood sucking attorneys"!).<p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 10-21-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroSTETZ||10-21-1999||03:57 PM||||the problem here is that i am only 17, and my insurance company (USAA) informed me that if i was to sue, it would go on my permanent insurance history and raise my rates.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mwbackus||10-21-1999||04:36 PM||||Keep in mind that driving without insurance is illegal, you can easily press charges against this person yourself, and request reimbursment from the court for any damages incurred that were not recieved as compensation from your insurance company.  It is unlikely that your insurance company would even know that a complaint had been filed.  This is exactly what I was suggesting.  It is important that the police be involved in any accident that causes considerable damage (the amount varies by state), primarily for the documention involved, but also as a check for a possibly intoxicated driver (ie.. even if the accident was your fault, if the other driver was intoxicated, they are completly liable for all damages).<P>Mike||||reg||1
